From: cvediver <cvediver@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: recover Tx queues stopped across
 a link flap

A Tx queue stopped on ring exhaustion is woken only from
edma_tx_complete(), and that wake is gated on netif_carrier_ok().  A
completion reaped after the carrier dropped therefore skips the wake;
once the ring has drained there are no further completions, so nothing
wakes the queue when the carrier returns.  The driver has no
ndo_tx_timeout, so dev_watchdog is never armed and cannot recover the
queue, and edma_port_open() starts only queue 0 (netif_start_queue), so
even an ifdown/ifup cycle leaves queues 1..n stopped forever.

Drop the carrier gate from the completion-side wake -- waking a queue
while the carrier is down is harmless since the qdisc is deactivated --
and start all Tx queues in edma_port_open() so that reopening the
device recovers any queue left stopped.

Signed-off-by: cvediver <cvediver@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.c
@@ -115,7 +115,13 @@
 	edma_cfg_tx_napi_enable(port_id);
 	edma_cfg_tx_enable_interrupts(port_id);
 
-	netif_start_queue(netdev);
+	/* Tx queues stopped on ring exhaustion are woken only by the Tx
+	 * completion path, which cannot run for a stopped queue whose ring
+	 * has already drained.  Start every queue on open so that an
+	 * ifdown/ifup cycle recovers queues left stopped by a link flap;
+	 * starting only queue 0 left the others stopped forever.
+	 */
+	netif_tx_start_all_queues(netdev);
 
 	return 0;
 }
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_tx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_tx.c
@@ -218,7 +218,12 @@
 
 	/* If tx_requeue_stop disabled (tx_requeue_stop = 0)
 	 * Fetch the tx queue paired with this Tx cmpl ring and check if it
-	 * is stopped. if queue is stopped and interface is up, wake it up.
+	 * is stopped. if queue is stopped, wake it up.  The wake must not
+	 * be gated on netif_carrier_ok(): a completion reaped while the
+	 * carrier is down is the last wake this queue will ever get -- the
+	 * ring is empty afterwards and nothing wakes the queue on
+	 * carrier-up, wedging it permanently.  Waking with the carrier down
+	 * is harmless; the qdisc is deactivated and nothing is transmitted.
 	 *
 	 * The queue must be derived from the ring, not from the CPU running
 	 * this completion handler: Tx desc and Tx cmpl rings are paired
@@ -237,8 +242,7 @@
 		queue_id = (txcmpl_ring->id - edma_ctx->hw_info->txcmpl->ring_start) %
 			   num_possible_cpus();
 		nq = netdev_get_tx_queue(txcmpl_ring->napi.dev, queue_id);
-		if (unlikely(netif_tx_queue_stopped(nq)) &&
-		    netif_carrier_ok(txcmpl_ring->napi.dev)) {
+		if (unlikely(netif_tx_queue_stopped(nq))) {
 			dev_dbg(dev, "Waking queue number %d, for interface %s\n",
 				queue_id, txcmpl_ring->napi.dev->name);
 			__netif_tx_lock(nq, smp_processor_id());
